# compromise_overlay.log — a SMALL, clearly-labeled teaching overlay appended to
# the REAL loghub OpenSSH log (data/OpenSSH_2k.log) at demo time ONLY.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: the real loghub OpenSSH_2k.log is almost entirely internet
# brute-force noise. It has exactly ONE successful login — `Accepted password
# for fztu from 119.137.62.142` (line ~956) — but that source IP never appears
# as a failure, so it's a BENIGN login, not a compromise. On its own the real
# log can't teach the core lesson ("a success from an IP that was ALSO
# brute-forcing is a compromise, not a coincidence") because no brute-force
# source ever succeeds. These two lines add exactly that one signal WITHOUT
# altering the real dataset — giving the learner a clean contrast (benign fztu
# vs. compromised jsmith): an `Accepted password` from
# 187.141.143.180 — the single most prolific brute-force source IP in the REAL
# log above. Same hostname (LabSZ) and `Dec 10` timeframe as the real entries so
# the overlay reads as one continuous log.
#
# WHY THE ACCOUNT IS `jsmith`: the success lands `jsmith` — a real account on the
# lab host that carries a NOPASSWD:ALL sudoers entry (see Dockerfile). That ties
# the log finding to passes 1 and 2: the brute-forced account is the one with
# total sudo, and the SUID-root backdoor (/usr/local/bin/.cache-helper) is what
# that access was used to plant. (The real loghub log brute-forces generic
# names; routing the synthetic success to an on-box privileged account is what
# makes "who got in → what can they do" resolve end to end.)
#
# This is the only synthetic content in the lab; everything else is the real
# public capture. Do not treat these two lines as real-world data.
Dec 10 09:45:11 LabSZ sshd[26041]: Accepted password for jsmith from 187.141.143.180 port 51330 ssh2
Dec 10 09:45:11 LabSZ sshd[26041]: pam_unix(sshd:session): session opened for user jsmith by (uid=0)
